window.docContents[54].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems","header":"5.4.2. Weak Elliptic Problems","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems"},"/Elliptic-Equations/Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Interior-Derivative-Estimates--Derivative-estimates-and-Liouville___s-theorem":{"contents":"Interior derivative estimates express the rigidity of harmonic functions\nquantitatively: the derivatives of  at a point are controlled by the size of\n on any surrounding sphere or ball. The proof identifies  with the\nPoisson extension of its boundary trace and differentiates the Poisson kernel\nat the center, which yields the constant  without assuming in advance\nthat the derivatives are harmonic.\n\nIn positive dimension, suppose , ,  is harmonic on a set\ncontaining the closed ball of radius  about , and\n on the boundary sphere. Then\n.\n\nTranslate the ball to the origin, extend the boundary trace, and identify the\ntranslated function locally with its Poisson extension.  Differentiating the\nPoisson kernel at the center and estimating the resulting spherical integral\nby Cauchy–Schwarz gives the explicit constant .\n\nReplacing the boundary supremum by an integral of  costs the volume of a\nhalf-radius ball: the boundary estimate is applied on , and  at\neach point of its sphere is bounded by the ball average of  over a ball of\nradius  that still lies inside . The constant\n produced this way is Evans's.\n\nIn positive dimension, if  and  is harmonic on a set containing\n, thenwhere  is the volume of the unit ball. The bound is for the\noperator norm of the Fréchet derivative, so it bounds each coordinate\nderivative.\n\nApply the boundary-supremum estimate on the half-radius ball.  At each point\nof its boundary sphere, apply the ball mean-value formula on the ball of\nradius  centered there. These smaller open balls lie in ;\nthe triangle inequality, monotonicity of the integral of , and\n give Evans's constant.\n\nIterating the first-order estimate bounds derivatives of every order, and each\niteration spends part of the available radius: to control  at , one\nestimates  on a sphere of radius  about , each point of\nwhich must still have a ball of radius  inside the domain.\nThe sum of radii records this nested geometry.\n\nIn positive dimension, let  be harmonic on an open set , let \nbe any natural number, and assign a positive radius budget  to each\nderivative step. IfthenTaking every  gives the uniform estimate on\n with denominator . In particular,  and\n recover the usual second- and third-derivative nested-ball estimates.\nThe order-zero case is included.\n\nFirst derive harmonicity of the evaluated derivatives from harmonicity of\n on the open domain. Then induct on , spending the head radius in\nthe final first-derivative estimate. On its boundary sphere, the induction\nhypothesis controls the preceding iterated derivative using the tail radii.\nApply the first-order estimate to each multilinear evaluation and use the\noperator norm to reassemble the directional bounds. Constant radius budgets\ngive the uniform theorem; replacing  by  gives the successor form.\n\nLiouville's theorem is the limiting case of the boundary-supremum estimate on\nall of : if  everywhere, then\n for every , so  vanishes identically\nand  is constant.\n\nIn positive dimension, if  is harmonic and\nglobally bounded, then\n\nApply the boundary-supremum derivative estimate on\narbitrarily large balls.  If the derivative norm were positive, choosing a\nradius larger than its bound would give a strict contradiction.  Mathlib's\nzero-derivative constancy theorem then identifies every value with the value\nat the origin.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity\u0009Interior Derivative Estimates","header":"5.3.3.1. Derivative estimates and Liouville's theorem","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Interior-Derivative-Estimates--Derivative-estimates-and-Liouville___s-theorem"},"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Chartwise-conormals-and-outward-unit-normals":{"contents":"Let  carry the finite boundary atlas used for\nSobolev extension, and assume . Each\nchart  flattens its boundary piece to a coordinate hyperplane.\nThe chart map and its inverse map are defined on the ambient space and have\nglobal  regularity, but they are inverse to one another only on their\nspecified chart neighborhoods. All geometric conclusions are restricted to the\nchart source.\n\nExtension uses these charts to reflect functions across the boundary. Here\nwe use their last coordinate to determine which direction points outward.\nDifferentiating that coordinate gives a conormal; proving that it never\nvanishes makes normalization possible. Agreement on overlapping charts then\nturns the local directions into one boundary normal.\n\nA graph makes the sign convention visible. Suppose locally that\n and flatten it by\n. The height  increases into the domain.\nIts differential sends a displacement  to\n. The conormal is this linear measurement of\nnormal displacement; the Euclidean inner product represents it by\n. Consequently the outward unit normal is\n\n\n\nFor the flat graph , this points straight down, out of the upper\nhalf-space. The general chart construction makes the same choice for charts\nof arbitrary form.\n\nFor  in the source of ,andHere  is represented by frontier Ω. These equivalences\nfix the inward/outward sign convention used in every subsequent normal\ndefinition.\n\nThe atlas axioms identify the part of  inside the chart source with\nthe preimage of the upper half-space, and identify the frontier there with the\npreimage of its boundary hyperplane. The upper half-space is characterized by\nstrict positivity of the last coordinate, while its boundary is characterized\nby vanishing of that coordinate. Specializing these two identifications at\n gives the claimed equivalences.\n\nInside a chart source the boundary is the zero level set of the chart height\n, so its normal direction at a\nboundary point should be the gradient of that height. The height is a scalar\nfunction on , its differential is a covector, and the Riesz map\nconverts the covector into a physical vector.\n\nLet  be last-coordinate projection. The differential\nof chart height at  is the continuous linear functionalThe physical chart conormal is its Riesz representative:Thus  for physical tangent vectors .\n\nA conormal is useful only if it can be normalized, which requires it to be\nnonzero. Invertibility of the chart enters here: differentiating\n at  shows that\n has a right inverse, and composing the nonzero functional\n with a surjective map cannot give zero.\n\nIf  belongs to the source of chart , then\n\nDifferentiate the local identity\n at . Hence\n has a right inverse. Apply this identity to the last basis\nvector . Ifthen . Therefore  is not the zero functional, and its\nRiesz representative  is nonzero.\n\nWith nonvanishing established on the chart source, normalization is legitimate\nthere.\n\nThe chartwise outward unit normal isThe chart models  by positive height. At a boundary point, chart height\nis zero, so the negative conormal points toward the exterior side of the\nmodel half-space.\n\nTwo facts justify the name outward unit normal: the height differential must\ntake a negative value on , and  must have norm one. Both\nrest on the Riesz identity  together\nwith , and the pairing can in fact be computed exactly.\n\nOn the source of chart , the chart-height differential evaluates on the\noutward normal as\n\nBy the Riesz identity,Apply linearity to  and cancel one norm\nfactor using .\n\nAt a boundary point, where chart height is zero, the sign of this value\nidentifies  as the outward normal to .\n\nThe chart height decreases strictly in the chartwise outward direction:\n\nUse\n. Nonvanishing of  gives\n, so its negative is strictly below zero.\n\nOrientation fixes the direction of ; its length remains. Dividing\nby  is a normalization only where that norm is\nnonzero, which again restricts the statement to the chart source.\n\nOn the source of chart ,\n\nNorm homogeneity givesThe conormal is nonzero, so its norm is nonzero and the product is one.\n\nEverything so far is relative to one chart. A point of  may\nlie in several chart sources, each producing its own conormal, and unless\nthese agree up to a positive factor no chart-independent normal can be\ndefined. Agreement of the kernels of the two height differentials gives a\ncommon tangent hyperplane and hence proportionality; the sign of the factor is\nwhere the shared height convention enters.\n\nSuppose  lies in the sources of charts  and .\nThen there is a strictly positive scalar  such thatThus the atlas gives not just a common tangent hyperplane but a coherent\nchoice of its outward side.\n\nFirst show that the two chart-height differentials vanish on exactly the same\nphysical vectors. A vector in the kernel of one height differential generates,\nto first order, a curve in that chart's flat boundary; chart overlap and the\nboundary-characterization theorem force the other height differential to\nvanish as well.Two nonzero linear functionals to  with the same kernel are scalar\nmultiples. To determine the sign, move from the boundary point along the\npositive last-coordinate ray in chart . Its inverse image lies inside\n, so chart  also assigns positive height. Passing to the\nright-hand derivative shows that the scalar is nonnegative. Nonvanishing of\nboth functionals rules out zero, hence the scalar is positive. Transport the\nfunctional equality through the Riesz equivalence.\n\nPositivity of the factor  is exactly what normalization needs: a negative\nfactor would leave the two unit normals opposite, while a positive one is\ncancelled by the norm in the denominator.\n\nAt a common boundary point of two chart sources,\n\nWrite  with . Then\n. The positive factor cancels in\n\nFor , choose a chart source containing  and defineThe finite atlas covers the frontier, and overlap compatibility proves that\nthe result does not depend on the chosen chart.\n\nA definition by an arbitrary choice is usable only through a lemma that\nreplaces the chosen chart by any convenient one.\n\nIf chart  contains the boundary point , then the intrinsic normal\nagrees with the chartwise normal:\n\nCompare the chart chosen in the definition of  with chart  and\napply chartwise overlap compatibility at .\n\nOnce the intrinsic normal can be computed in any chart containing the point,\nevery chartwise property transfers to it.\n\nFor every ,\n\nChoose a chart containing , replace the intrinsic normal by its chartwise\nrepresentative, and use the chartwise unit-norm theorem.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Boundary Calculus and C¹ Boundary Geometry","header":"3.3.2.6. Chartwise conormals and outward unit normals","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Chartwise-conormals-and-outward-unit-normals"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Exact-solid-rotation":{"contents":"Consider the planar fields\n\n\n\nThe velocity is divergence-free. Its convective acceleration is\n, while , so pressure supplies the\ncentripetal force. This flow is rotational rather than irrotational:\n and its Bernoulli function is .\nIt is constant along the circular streamlines, but not throughout the plane.\n\nWe reuse the polynomial differentiation from Foundations to check a vector\nequation and its pressure constraint. The pressure itself is not harmonic;\nits Laplacian balances the divergence of the convective acceleration. The\nsame velocity has zero strain, which connects this flow to the\nsymmetric-gradient energy example.\n\nThe verified CAS provesThe symbolic calculation produces the differentiated residual, while the\ngenerated theorem proves that the momentum and pressure residuals vanish on\nall of .\n\nThe divergence is . Direct differentiation\ngiveswhich cancels . The checked proof reflects these polynomial\nexpressions, differentiates them symbolically, and closes the normalized\nidentities with cas. It also verifies  and\n.\n\nThe exact momentum proof is a one-line tactic call:\n\n-- Prove the exact vector momentum residual is zero.\nopen CAS.Certificate\nopen NavierStokes.Symbolic\n\nexample :\n    ResidualBound\n      solidRotationEulerMomentumResidual2.vectorFn\n      Set.univ 0 := by\n  cas\n\n\nThe symbolic certificate yields the analytic statementsThese conclusions use the ordinary analytic operators\npartialDeriv, materialDerivative, and spatialGradient.\n\nThe zero-error certificates make each evaluated symbolic residual vanish.\nThe bridge lemmas identify those evaluations with the corresponding analytic\noperators. Since the certified vorticity is the constant , all of its\nspacetime derivatives vanish, giving the transport equation as well.\n\nThe same rigid rotation has zero strain:Thus the velocity gradient is antisymmetric, every strain entry vanishes,\nand the symmetric-gradient energy density is zero.\n\nThe four spatial derivatives are . In each strain component the\noff-diagonal derivatives cancel and the diagonal derivative is zero. The\ncertified energy expression is therefore a sum of zero squares.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow\u0009Incompressible Euler Flow","header":"8.4.1.7. Exact solid rotation","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Exact-solid-rotation"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure--Solenoidal-Spaces-and-Weak-Pressure--The-divergence-free-energy-space":{"contents":"Let  be a normed space of velocity fields, let  be a normed scalar\nspace, and let\n\n\n\nbe a continuous linear divergence operator. The domain, boundary condition,\nand Sobolev realization are left open.\n\nThe solenoidal energy space is the closed linear subspaceIt comes with its canonical continuous inclusion . Representing incompressibility as a kernel makes the constraint available\nto the general theory of submodules, weak limits, and restricted forms.\n\nA continuous linear kernel is weakly closed, so weak limits preserve the\ndivergence-free constraint. This is the closure property needed when passing\nfrom approximate solutions to a weak solution.\n\nThe set  is closed in the weak topology of . Consequently, ifand  eventually, then .\n\nRegard  as the one-member family of constraints in the abstract weak\nconstraint theorem. Its lifted weak-space kernel is closed, and every limit\nof an eventually kernel-valued net lies in that kernel.\n\nApproximate solutions of an evolution problem are trajectories\n rather than single vectors, and incompressibility is then\nimposed only for almost every time. Composing with  lifts the constraint to\na continuous linear map on Bochner , so the previous argument applies\nverbatim once the almost-everywhere condition is identified with the kernel of\nthat lift.\n\nFor , almost-everywhere incompressibility,is equivalent to vanishing of the lifted continuous map\n. Hence the solenoidal trajectory space is\nweakly closed, and weak  limits of eventually solenoidal trajectories\nremain solenoidal. If additionally  is a real Hilbert space, intersecting\nthis constraint set with any weakly closed, norm-bounded family is weakly\ncompact.\n\nThe Bochner lift agrees almost everywhere with pointwise composition by\n. Equality of Bochner classes identifies its kernel with\nalmost-everywhere vanishing, after which weak closedness of a continuous\nlinear kernel gives the limit theorem. The weak compactness theorem for\nclosed norm-bounded Hilbert-space families remains valid after intersecting\nwith this closed constraint set.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Solenoidal Spaces, Compactness, and Pressure\u0009Solenoidal Spaces and Weak Pressure","header":"8.3.1.1. The divergence-free energy space","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Solenoidal-Spaces___-Compactness___-and-Pressure--Solenoidal-Spaces-and-Weak-Pressure--The-divergence-free-energy-space"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Diffusion-and-weak-evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution--Why-the-heat-kernel-is-fundamental":{"contents":"The Gaussian kernel\n\n\n\nis the heat equation's response to a unit point source. You can derive it:\nparabolic scaling and radial symmetry cut a mass-preserving candidate down to an\nODE in , and solving that ODE and normalizing the mass gives the\nGaussian. Four properties are proved separately. It solves the heat equation\nfor . It carries unit mass  at every positive\ntime. It is strictly positive at every positive time. And as \nit concentrates into an approximate identity. Convolving against it,\n, therefore solves the equation for positive time,\nbecause differentiation passes under the integral onto the kernel, and recovers\n as , by the approximate-identity limit. The\ncertified plots of the computation chapter draw\n at three times as bands whose panels are kernel-checked\nenclosure theorems.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Diffusion and weak evolution","header":"6.1.2. Why the heat kernel is fundamental","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Diffusion-and-weak-evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution--Why-the-heat-kernel-is-fundamental"}});